import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const diagCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Verify the complaint', note: 'Drive or reproduce the fault first. "It makes a noise" is a story; a repeatable symptom is a case.' },
    { math: '2 · Read, then measure', note: 'Service information and DTCs (fault codes) point toward systems — never at a part. The scanner names suspects; tests convict them.' },
    { math: '3 · Test the circuit, not the part', note: 'Voltage drop tests, continuity, reference signals: components fail in patterns, and the wiring between them fails more often than the parts.' },
    { math: '4 · Repair the root cause', note: 'A blown fuse is a symptom. Replacing it without finding why it blew is the trade\'s definition of a comeback.' },
    { math: '5 · Verify and document', note: 'Re-test after repair, then write what was found, fixed and verified. Documentation is what turns a repair into a service history.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/automotive-technology/foundational/shop-safety-and-service-basics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Automotive technology is a diagnostic discipline wrapped around mechanical craft: modern
    vehicles are networks of sensors and computers bolted to engines and brakes. The trade's
    foundations are shop safety, service information literacy and a diagnostic method that
    replaces parts-swapping with proof.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the diagnostic method, from complaint to comeback" widget="equationStep" config={diagCfg} />

  <h2>Safety systems are not suggestions</h2>
  <p>
    The shop's killer hazards are ranked and engineered against: vehicle lifting (jack stands
    always, never hydraulics alone), fuel vapour and battery arcs, rotating belts, hot coolant —
    and, in the modern bay, high-voltage orange cables that demand certified EV training before
    touch. Squirting brake cleaner near a spark source or working under an unsupported vehicle are
    the two classics every safety module opens with, because both have memorials.
  </p>

  <h2>Service information is half the job</h2>
  <p>
    No technician memorises a modern car. Manufacturers publish torque specs, wiring diagrams,
    relearn procedures and technical service bulletins — and finding the right page quickly is a
    tested skill (ASE includes it). The professional habit: verify the spec before the repair and
    read the bulletin before the diagnosis, because someone may have solved this complaint already.
  </p>

  <h2>The modern vehicle is a computer network</h2>
  <p>
    Dozens of ECUs converse on CAN buses; a stiff steering feel may be a calibration, a rough idle
    may be a sensor's dirty reference voltage. That is why the trade now teaches electronics
    alongside engines — and why the best technicians describe themselves as diagnosticians first,
    with the wrench as one instrument among several.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASE Education Foundation program standards; ASE A1–A9 test content; manufacturer service information systems.</p>
</TopicPage>
